  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA near surface Pacific cold front accompanies the mid to upper \ndisturbance as it develops into the Southern Great Plains Monday. \nSoutherly winds shift to westerly behind the front and increase \nabove 40 to 50 mph over the Guadalupes/Delawares, with gusts up to\n65 mph indicated in forecast grids. These winds persist through \nmuch of Monday afternoon/evening. The duration of strong winds has\nprompted a High Wind Watch for the Guadalupes/Delawares and \nsurrounding passes during that time frame. Not out of the question\nthat some patchy blowing dust during strong winds may occur as \nwell near the Guadalupes/Delawares and surrounding foothills, \ndespite recent rains. Otherwise, 25 to 35 mph winds can also be \nexpected for the plains/lowlands to the east behind the Pacific \ncold front, with low (25% to 35%) rain chances and warmer \ntemperatures in the 70s and 80s F ahead of the cold front for the \neastern Permian Basin into Terrell County. Due to the more \nprogressive and zonal upper air pattern that follows the \ndisturbance, the modified Pacific air will only result in highs \ndropping down into the 60s to lower 70s F, lows mid 40s to mid 50s\nF behind the front. \n\nWith the front clearing the area Tuesday and winds shifting to \nnorthwesterly/northerly and decreasing, continued CAA brings highs\nback down to the 60s to 70s F range in the mild, terrain modified\nPacific airmass. However, with a progressive upper air pattern \nkeeping alternating ridging and troughing aloft through end of the\nweek, highs and lows continue to fluctuate within the 60s to 70s \nF and mid 40s to mid 50s F range each day and night, respectively.\nAfter the relatively humid and rainy pattern, rain chances will \nremain near zero for the rest of the week. The combination of the \nmild air, greater diurnal variation, and longer nights will be in \nsync with seasonal norms for early November.",
